(* ============================================================================
   9. EMPHASIS SPAN RESOLUTION -- FORMAL GUARDS + DELIMITER-STACK
      OPERATIONAL SEMANTICS (governs the *_content productions in PART 3;
      §1 points here). The per-delimiter boundary tests ARE the
      bare_opener(d) / bare_closer(d) templates in PART 3. Run resolution
      is one left-to-right pass over the inline stream with a delimiter
      stack -- O(n), NO backtracking (Design Principle 1):
      E1 CLASSIFY. At each unescaped bare delimiter d outside code spans /
         raw inline, evaluate bare_opener(d) and bare_closer(d). Neither
         holds -> literal text. A d followed by a grapheme_extender is a
         part of a larger character and satisfies neither (CARVE-P3-025):
         `*a*` + U+20DD is literal, `*a*` + U+0301 + ` b*` pairs the
         first `*` with the last, and a spacing mark counts the same:
         `*` + U+093E + `x *y*` leaves the first `*` text.
      E2 CLOSE FIRST. A d satisfying bare_closer(d) with a REACHABLE open
         d-entry on the stack (E2a) closes the NEAREST such entry. Spans NEST,
         never overlap: entries pushed above the closed one are popped and
         their delimiters demoted to literal (the djot model -- an opener that
         gets closed demotes the still-open candidates between).
      E2a OPAQUE CONSTRUCTS. No delimiter -- bare, a braced closer or the
         substitution arrow `~>` -- pairs across the boundary of a CODE SPAN
         (with raw inline and math), a BRACED INLINE (§22's forced spans,
         `{+ +}`, `{- -}`, `{~ ~> ~}`, `{# #}`, `{% %}`), a LINK DESTINATION (title
         included), an AUTOLINK or a CROSS-REFERENCE (`</#id>`), in either
         direction; they are atomic (ruled 2026-10-15). Inside a braced inline
         only its own entries are reachable, and open ones demote at its closer.
         `~{/x/}{/y~/}` -> ~<em>x</em><em>y~</em>.
      E2b BRACKET-RUN BOUND. PART 8 resolves a BRACKET RUN before bare
         emphasis, forced spans and non-opaque editorial markup. Every delimiter pair,
         bare or braced, must open and close inside the same run. An opener
         inside a run cannot pair with a closer outside it, even when the run
         resolves to literal text rather than a link or span:
         `[{+a]+}` and `[{^a]^}` are literal text. This bounds pairing, not
         label content: a complete braced span inside a label still parses.
         CARVE-P3-001 defines which constructs the bracket scan skips.
         In particular, a bracket inside an editorial comment opens no run,
         so `[{#a]#}`
         retains its comment and renders the outside `[`.
      E3 NO BARE SAME-TYPE NESTING. While a reachable d-span is open,
         a further bare d that would open does NOT push -- it is literal.
         Hence /usr/local/ -> <em>usr/local</em> (01-emphasis-6);
         the/path/here is literal; unbalanced trailing runs stay literal:
         /b// -> <em>b</em>/ and /x// -> <em>x</em>/ (resources/examples/edge-cases.md).
         An explicit braced opener is exempt: it MAY push a child of the
         same kind under either a bare or a braced parent (§22). Each braced
         span creates the E2a boundary; its closer closes that span, never
         an ancestor across an intervening braced boundary. Thus
         {*a {*b*} c*} nests, while {*a *b* c*} keeps the inner bare stars
         literal. Cross-type behavior is unchanged: {*a {/b {*c*} d/} e*}
         nests, and a *} inside the {/ /} closes nothing outside it.
      E4 OPEN. Otherwise a d satisfying bare_opener(d) pushes an entry.
      E5 END OF BLOCK. Entries still open demote to literal.
      Forced `{X … X}` spans (§22) push/pop on the SAME stack; only the
      boundary tests are skipped.
      CONSEQUENCES (pinned): SAME-DELIMITER ADJACENCY is bare_opener's
      <!(d) / !(d) guards -- a doubled bare delimiter is literal, never
      nested same-type emphasis: `**x**`, `~~x~~`, `==x==` like `//x//`,
      `__x__` (corpus 76-doubled-emphasis-delimiters, 01-emphasis-11);
      longer runs (`===y===`) all-literal. (The braced-only `^`/`,` are not
      bare, so `^^x^^` and `,,x,,` are likewise literal.) SLASH-ADJACENCY
      (slash_if(d), bare_opener's extra left guard): italic `/` and underline
      `_` do NOT open when the immediately preceding char is `/`, so `a_/_a_`,
      `a/_y_` and `/a/_b_` -> <em>a</em>_b_ stay literal at the `_`; for `/`
      this is same-delimiter adjacency. The other delimiters `* ~ =` are NOT
      guarded against a preceding `/` (a/~y~ -> a/<s>y</s>, a/=y= ->
      a/<mark>y</mark>) (corpus 129-emphasis-opener-slash-adjacency). WORD BOUNDARY: (/x/) ->
      (<em>x</em>), a./b/ -> a.<em>b</em> (01-emphasis-8); foo_bar_baz,
      foo*bar*baz, snake_case, x = 5, key=value literal (01-emphasis-10,
      -11); "x /a/b y" literal -- the candidate closer is followed by `b`
      (01-emphasis-9). Intraword emphasis: forced family only, x{*y*}z ->
      x<strong>y</strong>z (§22).

   10. PARAGRAPH INTERRUPTION -- OPERATIONAL SEMANTICS (governs
      `paragraph` in PART 2). The relation interrupts(L, P) decides whether
      a line L ends an OPEN paragraph P with NO blank line between, at the
      document top level AND inside nested content (list item, block quote,
      admonition/div body). L interrupts -> L is parsed as its block and P
      ends on the preceding line. PART 0 O1 handles opaque payloads and
      comment tokens first; O2 selects the owner; O3 and O4 use the same
      recognition result in that owner's coordinates and permitted scope.
      I1 to I7 specify interruption policy, not a second block recognizer.
      A recognized opener need not interrupt: list markers follow I2, fence
      guards follow I4, and caption attachment follows I7. PART 0 X1 to X3
      retain their ownership and folding exceptions.
      I1 VISIBLE OPENERS interrupt. interrupts(L, P) holds when L begins:
         - a heading (`#`..`######` + space);
         - a thematic break (a lone `---` / `***` / `___` line);
         - a block quote (`>` alone or `> `; `>text`, `>= 5`, and `>>=`
           are prose, not quote markers);
         - a definition-list term (`:: ` followed by non-whitespace content);
         - a valid table row (valid_row(L), §5 T2; a stray `|` in prose,
           and a line-initial `|` with no closing `|`, are NOT ones);
         - a fenced code opener WITH a matching closer ahead (I4);
         - a `:::` opener, closer or not (I4).
         So "intro \n # H" is <p>intro</p> + <h1>; "intro \n --- \n more"
         is <p>intro</p> + <hr> + <p>more</p> (corpus
         81-paragraph-interruption).
      I2 LIST MARKERS NEVER INTERRUPT (symmetric). NEITHER a bullet
         (`- `/`* `, `- [x] ` task) NOR an ordered marker (decimal, letter,
         roman, any value) interrupts: the line FOLDS into P as lazy
         continuation; a list needs a blank line before it (matching Djot).
         "Liste: \n - eins \n - zwei" is ONE paragraph, so is
         "x = 5 \n * 3 + 17"; a blank line starts the list.
         The only exception is the one-line paragraph formed by a refused
         sibling `+` under §17 L3. A following list item may interrupt it.
         TIGHT NESTED LISTS UNAFFECTED:
         indented marker inside an open list ITEM opens a sublist with no
         blank line -- that is §24 C3 (content column), not this relation.
      I3 IMAGE EXCLUDED. A line that is only a bare image ("![alt](url)")
         does not interrupt: an image is not a block of its own in Carve;
         it stays an inline image inside the paragraph.
      I4 CLOSER LOOKAHEAD -- the same &(...) the VERBATIM fence productions
         carry. An UNTERMINATED ``` or ~~~ opener does not interrupt; the line
         stays paragraph text, and a stray ``` in prose then opens an unclosed
         inline verbatim run rendering as <code> to the end of the block (the
         code_span maximal-run rule). Pinned by corpus
         81-paragraph-interruption-19.

         A `:::` OPENER IS NOT GUARDED. It interrupts whether or not a closer
         follows; where an unterminated one closes is PART 2's UNCLOSED rule
         (`admonition`). Pinned by corpus 81-paragraph-interruption-20: `Text` /
         `:::` / `stuff` is a paragraph followed by a div holding `stuff`.

      I5 INVISIBLE CONSTRUCTS interrupt AND are consumed: a reference
         definition (link `[r]: url`, footnote `[^r]: …`, abbreviation
         `*[A]: …`), a comment (`%%` line, `%%%` block), and a
         block-attribute line (`{…}` alone on a line, §15). "See[^m].\n
         [^m]: note" resolves the footnote; "para\n%% x" drops the comment;
         "Para\n{.x}" ends the paragraph with the attribute line floating
         forward (§15; corpus 89-block-attribute-lines-7). (A small
         deviation from djot, which keeps such a line as paragraph text.)
         AN INVISIBLE LINE IS ONE CLASSIFICATION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-013]. The five kinds
         above are one class, and it is MEMBERSHIP in that class the rest of
         the grammar cites. An invisible line, in the container that owns it:

           1. CLOSES an open paragraph, with no blank line needed -- this
              clause, and I6 for the containers;
           2. EMITS NO VISIBLE BLOCK at its authored location: nothing renders
              where the line was written, not an empty paragraph, not a blank
              line, not residual whitespace (L6 for the definitions, §21 and
              §28 for the comments, §15 A2 for the attribute line);
           3. DOES NOT ITSELF CHOOSE THE OWNER of the line that follows it. It
              is neither a paragraph nor a separator, so it can neither BE the
              next block (A2a) nor stand between a blank line and the block
              after it (L1b).

         ONLY THE SEMANTIC ACTION DIFFERS BY KIND, and that action is the only
         thing a per-kind clause has left to state: a comment DISCARDS its
         visible content (§21, §28), an attribute line RETAINS pending
         metadata in the current container (§15 A2), and a definition
         REGISTERS metadata in its permitted scope (L6, PART 9R). A clause that
         restates properties 1 to 3 for one kind is restating this one; a clause
         that states an action, a pipeline ORDER, a source-map overlap or column
         ARITHMETIC is not, and stays where it is.

         PROPERTY 3 DOES NOT SAY WHICH OWNER. PART 0 O2 to O4 select it;
         X1 alone specifies retained ownership after a below-column comment
         (CARVE-P9-053). Corpus 189 and 192 pin the list-item cases.
         The exemptions below decide membership, not the follower's owner.

         THE CLASSIFICATION HAS EXACTLY TWO EXCEPTIONS, and both are about
         MEMBERSHIP -- which lines are in the class at all -- never about a
         member behaving differently:

           - A COMMENT IS COLUMN-EXEMPT (A COMMENT IS THE ONE EXCEPTION, §24
             C3). Below a container's content column a comment is still
             invisible and still closes the paragraph. The other four kinds are
             ordinary text there, and they fold into the container that holds
             the open paragraph, in every host (AN INVISIBLE LINE FOLDS LIKE ANY
             OTHER; WHICH CONTAINER, NOT ONLY THAT IT IS TEXT; corpus 430).
           - AN ABBREVIATION DEFINITION IS DOCUMENT-LEVEL ONLY (CARVE-P12-026).
             Anywhere else it is not an invisible line at all: it is paragraph
             text, it is VISIBLE, and it folds like any other plain line. That
             is a line OUTSIDE the class, not a third band inside it.

         Comments in this list are the layout-transparent tokens classified in
         PART 0 before visible ownership. They close the paragraph without
         choosing or extending an owner; their AST/source extent is retained.

         DEFINITION OWNERSHIP IS COLUMN-SCOPED. A link or footnote definition
         belongs to an open list item only at that item's content_column, or
         when L3's `+` marker explicitly attaches its flush-left block. At
         DOCUMENT column 0 it is a document-level interrupter: it ends the item,
         is collected into the shared symbol table, and the following block is
         a document sibling. At a nonzero column BELOW content_column it is lazy
         paragraph text of THAT container -- the one whose content column it
         fell below -- and does not register. Comment recognition is not an
         exception to this opener set: PART 0 has already removed comment
         tokens from visible block classification. Pinned by corpus
         287-a-column-zero-definition-ends-an-open-list-item.

         THE COLUMN SCOPE IS THE SAME FOR AN ATTRIBUTE LINE, and the three
         bands are the same three bands. A block-attribute line is
         on I5's interrupter list with the definitions, and §15 A2's pending
         metadata is scoped to a container exactly as a definition's collection
         is: at DOCUMENT column 0 it ends the container and floats forward at
         document level, at content_column it is the container's own pending
         metadata, and at a nonzero column below it is lazy paragraph text that
         attaches nothing. Only the SEMANTIC ACTION differs by kind -- register
         in scope versus retain pending metadata -- and the column that selects
         the band does not.

         WHICH CONTAINER, NOT ONLY THAT IT IS TEXT. "Lazy paragraph text" names
         an operation on an OPEN PARAGRAPH, so the text lands in the container
         that holds that paragraph; a reader that ends the container and then
         publishes the same characters one level out has produced text in a
         place the sentence does not name. Pinned per kind by corpus
         430-below-a-definition-body-s-column-an-invisible-line-folds-as-text.

         A DEFINITION COLLECTED AT content_column ENDS THE ITEM'S OPEN
         PARAGRAPH (CARVE-P0-010). The next nonblank line therefore stays in
         the item only if it reaches content_column and opens item prose. A
         footnote definition's own extent is CARVE-P0-012; a line one column
         short of its body is item prose. The comment exception above does not
         extend to definitions. The bare decimal-dot marker `. ` claims one
         marker column and one separator column, so its content_column is the
         same as `- `. Pinned by corpus 374.
      I6 SCOPE. The relation applies to EVERY open
         paragraph, including a blockquote's lazy continuation: "> quoted
         \n - item" folds into ONE quote whose paragraph is
         "quoted\n- item" (NOT quote + <ul>); "> text \n > # H" is a quoted
         heading; a quoted bullet ("> p \n > - x") folds the same way.
         A heading is not an open paragraph. Its SINGLE-LINE HEADINGS rule
         in PART 2 determines its extent; T's heading transition leaves no
         paragraph or continuation claim (CARVE-P0-006). Thus "# H \n - item"
         starts a sibling list without invoking paragraph interruption.
      I7 A CAPTION LINE DOES NOT INTERRUPT. A `^ ` line appears in neither
         I1 nor I5. What ends a paragraph at a caret is §4, and §4 reaches
         only the seven captionable hosts; for the two it spells in prose that
         is a paragraph whose WHOLE content is one image or one display-math
         span, and there the caret line ends the paragraph and ATTACHES.
         Anywhere else it folds in as ordinary paragraph text, caret and all:
         "Text \n ^ cap" is one paragraph, and so is
         "Text \n ![a](x.png) \n ^ cap", where the image is not the whole
         paragraph and so there is no host to attach to.
         NOT IN CONFLICT WITH THE CAPTION'S OWN RULE. A `^ ` line DOES end an
         open CAPTION (PART 2, MULTI-LINE CAPTIONS, item 4) -- that is a rule
         about a caption, stated separately from item 2's "a line that
         interrupts a paragraph" precisely because a caret line is not one of
         those. Item 4 would be redundant if it were.
         An INDENTED caret line opens no top-level block at all, so both
         readings agree there.

   11. LIST MARKER CHANGE STARTS A NEW LIST -- OPERATIONAL (governs `list`
      in PART 2). §10 decides FIRST whether marker lines establish a list
      at all (the interruption gate); §11 then partitions established
      same-indent items into sibling lists. The rules layer; §11 never
      overrides §10.
      N1 SAME LIST iff two adjacent items at the same indent match on ALL
         axes: bullet character (`-` vs `*`; `+` is NOT a bullet in Carve --
         it is the continuation marker, §17), ordered_marker alternative,
         delimiter (`.` vs `)`), and plain-vs-task classification. Any axis
         differs -> a new sibling list at the same indent. Matches djot
         (minus djot's `+` bullet). Example:
           - a              produces:    <ul><li>a</li><li>b</li></ul>
           - b                            <ul><li>c</li><li>d</li></ul>
           * c
           * d
         `- a` followed by `- [x] b` is likewise two lists.
         N1a HARD BOUNDARY AFTER THREE BLANK LINES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-014]. A run of
         THREE OR MORE blank lines before an item marker ends the list: the
         marker after the run opens a NEW sibling list even when every N1
         axis matches. ONE blank line remains the ordinary loose separator,
         and so does TWO (§17 L1). In the common LF spelling the boundary is
         four newline bytes between the two item lines (`item\n\n\n\nitem`)
         against two for the tight spelling and three for the loose one.
         Example:
           - apples         produces:    <ul><li>apples</li></ul>
                                         <ul><li>oranges</li></ul>
           (two more blank lines)
           - oranges
         AT EVERY LEVEL. The boundary is not restricted to the top level: it
         applies wherever a list is, including a list nested in a list item
         and a list inside a quote or a div.
         THE RUN CLOSES NOTHING ON ITS OWN. It denies a FOLLOWING SIBLING
         MARKER the right to join; it does not end the item. A run followed
         by a continuation at the item's content column continues that item,
         at any run length -- which is §17's content-column model unchanged,
         and is why three blank lines before indented text still leave one
         loose item.
         Two blank lines remain ordinary decorative spacing; three establish
         the boundary.
      N2 ORDERED DIALECT. A list's dialect (decimal / lower- or upper-alpha
         / lower- or upper-roman) and delimiter are fixed by its FIRST
         item; a later marker outside the dialect, or with the other
         delimiter, starts a new sibling list. The first item also sets the
         `<ol>` `type` (a/A/i/I; decimal omits it) and `start` (the
         marker's value; omitted when 1).
      N3 AMBIGUOUS-LETTER TIE-BREAK. A single roman-letter first marker
         (i/v/x/l/c/d/m) is ROMAN when the next sibling is the consecutive
         roman numeral (`iv.`+`v.`, `i.`+`ii.`) and ALPHA when the next is
         the consecutive letter (`c.`+`d.`, `v.`+`w.`); a lone `i`/`I`
         defaults to roman, any other lone letter to alpha.
      N1 looks only one item back; N2/N3 carry the FIRST item's
      classification (with N3's one-item-forward tie-break) for the rest of
      the list.

   12. FENCED ":::" BLOCK RENDERING -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-015] (governs `admonition`
      and `div` in PART 2; cross-references syntax.md §4.20 "Block
      Extensions"). A TYPED `::: word` block renders by a TWO-TIER rule
      on the type identifier (below). A BARE `:::` opener with NO type
      word is a GENERIC DIV: a plain `<div>` (no class added), i.e. djot's
      generic container. An EMPTY one closes on the next line --
      `<div>` newline `</div>`, the exception PART 10 §4 states.

      INLINE OPENER ATTRIBUTES. None. The opener's shape and the recovery of
      a malformed one are PART 2's (STRICT, under `admonition_open`). To
      attribute a div or admonition, use a PRECEDING block-attribute line,
      which floats onto the block (§15): `{.x #id}` then `::: note` yields
      `<aside class="admonition note x" id="id" aria-label="Note">`.

      FENCE LENGTH, NESTING, DIRECTION AND AN UNCLOSED OPENER are stated once,
      in PART 2 at `colon_fence_close` and under `admonition`, for admonitions
      and generic divs alike (corpus
      271-the-flush-left-line-after-a-container-a-quoted-line-opened).

      A COLON-FENCE LINE THAT FAILS THE OPENER TEST LEAVES THE PARAGRAPH
      EXPECTING A CLOSER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-016]. A `:::` line that is not a valid opener
      (an attribute block where the opener takes a type word, say) is ordinary
      paragraph text, and from that point the paragraph absorbs the next
      fence-shaped line as text too, INSTEAD of being interrupted by it:

          text                    ::: {.x}
          not a div               not a div
          :::                     :::

      The left column is a paragraph followed by an empty div, because a bare
      `:::` interrupts an open paragraph (§10). The right column is ONE
      paragraph holding all three lines. The malformed line need not be the
      paragraph's first.

      THE ABSORPTION IS NOT WIDTH-TAGGED, unlike the closer rule it resembles:
      after a malformed `::: {.x}`, a following `::::` is absorbed too, and
      after a malformed `:::: {.x}` so is a following `:::`. A malformed opener
      has no length to match against, so there is nothing for a width test to
      compare; the paragraph simply continues.

      ABSORPTION REACHES A PARAGRAPH'S OWN LINES ONLY -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-017].
      The absorbed line has to be one the paragraph's container
      supplied a prefix for. A FLUSH-LEFT fence-shaped line under a QUOTED
      paragraph is not:

          > quote
          > :::note
          > body
          :::

      `:::note` has no separator, so it opens nothing and is absorbed, and the
      QUOTED `> :::` under it would be absorbed on the same terms. The `:::`
      above supplies no `>` prefix: it is a PARTIAL match, and it could reach
      that paragraph only through PART 0 O3's lazy fold. The strict column-0
      rule decides it instead -- a flush-left fence-shaped line interrupts, the
      quote closes, and the line is re-classified at top level, where it opens
      a div of its own. Change one line to `> :::` and the answer inverts,
      because then it IS one of the paragraph's lines.

      FENCE OPENER WITH A NESTED-LIST BODY: a `:::` opener inside a LIST
      ITEM opens its block even when the opener's body is a nested list
      (`- item` / `1. item`). The matching closer is located at the ITEM
      CONTENT COLUMN (the column the opener starts at), not at column zero;
      a `:::` at column zero is outside the item and does NOT close it. With
      a valid closer, the bullet/ordered marker lines are the admonition's
      body and the block wraps the nested `<ul>`/`<ol>`. A blank line
      between the opener and the nested list still opens the block, and an
      empty body (opener immediately followed by its closer) opens too. A
      MISSING closer changes none of this: PART 2's UNCLOSED rule governs
      (corpus 270-a-real-div-in-a-container-and-the-flush-left-line-after-it).
      (resources/examples/edge-cases.md "Fence opener with a nested-list body
      inside a list item" pins these.)

      The two tiers for a typed block:

      Tier 1 -- CANONICAL ADMONITION TYPES render as a semantic
        `<aside>` with the `admonition` marker class:
            <aside class="admonition {type}">
              [<p class="admonition-title">{quoted_title}</p>]
              {body}
            </aside>
        The canonical set is the eight call-out types
        `note`, `tip`, `warning`, `danger`, `info`, `success`,
        `example`, `quote`. Consuming CSS frameworks target these exact
        class names. A type is a keyword, not a name, and matches
        exactly: `::: Note` is not the `note` admonition but the Tier-2
        `<div class="Note">`.

      Tier 2 -- ANY OTHER (custom) type renders as a generic block-level
        `<div>` carrying the verbatim type as its class:
            <div class="{type}">
              [<p class="admonition-title">{quoted_title}</p>]
              {body}
            </div>
        This is the carve fenced-div primitive that the block-extension
        mechanism (syntax.md §4.20) builds on -- e.g. `::: tabs`,
        `::: mermaid`, `::: codepen` produce `<div class="tabs">`,
        `<div class="mermaid">`, `<div class="codepen">` respectively,
        which a registered extension may post-process. An unregistered
        custom type still renders as its generic `<div class="{type}">`
        so the document stays readable. `details` is an ordinary Tier-2
        type (`<div class="details">`); an extension that wants the
        HTML5 `<details>/<summary>` disclosure element opts in via §4.20.

      Pinned rules common to both tiers:
        a. The class is the literal type identifier (Tier 1 prefixes it
           with `admonition ` separated by a single space:
           `admonition note`, NOT `admonition-note`; Tier 2 uses the
           bare `{type}`). The type is NEVER folded together with the
           quoted title -- the title is a child element, never a class.
        b. A `<p class="admonition-title">…</p>` line is emitted ONLY
           when the author supplied a `quoted_title` after the type.
           Carve does NOT invent a default title from the type name.
           An explicitly empty `""` still counts as a supplied (empty)
           title. Applies to both tiers.

      AN ADMONITION LANDMARK CARRIES AN ACCESSIBLE NAME -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-018]. A
      Tier-1 block renders `<aside>`, which is a COMPLEMENTARY LANDMARK, and a
      landmark with no name is an anonymous row in a reader's landmark list.
      Tier 2 renders `<div>`, is not a landmark, and takes nothing here.

        TITLED: the name is the title the author already wrote. The
        `<p class="admonition-title">` takes an `id` and the aside points at it
        with `aria-labelledby`, so the visible name and the accessible name are
        ONE string that cannot drift apart:
            <aside class="admonition warning" aria-labelledby="adm-1">
              <p class="admonition-title" id="adm-1">Careful</p>

        UNTITLED: there is no visible name to point at, so the name is the TYPE
        WORD, drawn from the `labels` map (§16a) rather than fixed English:
            <aside class="admonition note" aria-label="Note">

        The minted id starts at `adm-{n}`, n counting Tier-1 titled blocks in
        document order from 1. Reserve explicit and automatic heading ids
        first, then add a duplicate suffix (`adm-1-2`, `adm-1-3`, ...) until
        the minted id is unused. Titled nameable directives use this same
        sequence (CARVE-P9-072).

        THE AUTHOR'S OWN NAME WINS, MATCHED CASE-INSENSITIVELY. When the
        attribute line carries either `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` under
        ANY ASCII casing -- an author's attribute NAME is emitted verbatim and
        HTML attribute names are case-insensitive, so `ARIA-LABEL` is the same
        attribute -- the engine writes NEITHER attribute and mints no id: a second naming attribute beside the author's leaves
        the name undefined, and the author's is the more specific statement.
        The title `<p>` is still emitted, unchanged, without an id.

      A DIRECTIVE'S TITLE AND LABEL ACCOMPANY THE REGION IT PLACES -- NORMATIVE
      [CARVE-P9-072]. A `directive` (PART 12 §35) places generated content: a
      `<section role="doc-endnotes">` for `footnotes`, a `<nav class="toc">` for
      `toc`, whatever element the Tier-3 extension for another kind emits. The
      opener's quoted title and its `[label]` render in that order. When the
      placed element admits paragraphs, they are its FIRST CHILDREN, in the
      two slots an admonition already uses. Where it does not admit paragraphs,
      they precede the generated element as specified below:

            <section role="doc-endnotes" aria-labelledby="adm-1">
              <p class="admonition-title" id="adm-1">Notes</p>
              <p class="div-label">End</p>
              <hr>
              <ol>...</ol>
            </section>

      NOTHING HERE IS A NEW SHAPE. A title is emitted only when authored,
      including an explicitly empty one, and never as a class; where the
      placed element admits paragraphs it is a child. A titled
      nameable region uses `aria-labelledby` at the title's minted id;
      an untitled one uses the `labels` map (§16a `endnotes`, TOC `tocNav`).
      The author's own `aria-label` or `aria-labelledby` still
      wins, matched case-insensitively, preventing id minting
      WHERE THE MARKER'S ATTRIBUTES REACH
      THE PLACED ELEMENT: they reach the TOC nav, but none reaches the
      endnotes section; any OTHER `aria-*`
      the author writes leaves the naming alone.
      The label renders as `<p class="div-label">` when no group extension
      consumes it. An untitled, unlabeled marker emits neither paragraph.
      Non-HTML targets follow CARVE-P11-032 T1 for both tokens.

      A SIBLING TITLE WAS REFUSED FOR A NAMEABLE REGION. Put the visible title
      inside a region that admits it, and use it as that region's accessible
      name rather than the map default.

      THE SEPARATOR DOES NOT MOVE. `footnotes` emits title, label, `<hr>`,
      then `<ol>`; a title does not suppress the separator.

      THE ID COMES FROM THE ADMONITION SEQUENCE, NOT A SECOND ONE. Titled
      nameable directives take THE NEXT n IN
      THAT SAME SEQUENCE as `adm-{n}`, in document order through the same slug registry.

      A DEGRADED MARKER CARRIES THEM TOO, WITHOUT THE NAME. A marker placing
      nothing renders `<div class="{kind}">`, with both tokens inside even
      when otherwise empty: this includes absent notes, a second placement
      marker, and an unregistered Tier-3 kind. Naming is PROHIBITED on role `generic`;
      a plain div takes NEITHER naming attribute and mints NO id; only the nameable placed regions,
      the endnotes section or TOC nav use the naming rule.

      WHERE THE ELEMENT CANNOT HOLD A PARAGRAPH, THE TOKENS PRECEDE IT.
      `glossary` places one `<dl>` per authored definition list; `index`
      places a `<ul>`. Emit title then label BEFORE their content and mint no
      id, without adding wrappers. For `index`, authored body blocks follow those tokens and precede the
      generated `<ul>`. `footnotes`, `toc` and
      degraded divs instead take tokens as children because they admit flow
      content; only the endnotes section and TOC nav can carry a name.

   13. HEADING SECTION WRAPPING -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-019] (governs `atx_heading` in
      PART 2 and the renderer; matches djot, see `jgm/djot` official
      `headings.test`). Every heading emits a `<section id="{id}">`
      wrapper around itself and the following content up to the next
      same-or-shallower heading. CARVE-P2-004 computes the heading id; this
      clause places it on the `<section>` element, NOT on the
      `<h*>` element.
      Algorithm (one stateful pass over top-level blocks):
        let openSections : Stack of (level, sectionElement) = []
        for each top-level child node:
          if node is Heading at level N:
            while openSections is non-empty AND top.level >= N:
              emit </section>; pop openSections
            emit <section id="{slug}">; push (N, ...)
            emit <h{N}>{inline-rendered children}</h{N}>
          else:
            emit node normally
        while openSections is non-empty:
          emit </section>; pop openSections
      Properties:
      - Adjacent same-level headings: produce sibling sections at the
        same level (`# A / # B` -> two <section>s).
      - Skipped levels: a `# H1 / ### H3` sequence nests H3's section
        inside H1's, because the stack-close test above (top.level
        >= N) only closes sections at level >= 3 (none open at level 3
        or 5/6). The structure mirrors djot's behavior; carve does NOT
        synthesize intermediate <h2>/<section> nodes.
      - Explicit `{#id}` on a heading: the id still lives on the
        `<section>`, not on the `<h*>`. The dedup namespace (syntax.md
        §4.1 / heading-id-tracker rules) is unchanged -- explicit ids are
        reserved before auto-ids in document order.
      - ATTRIBUTES OTHER THAN THE ID STAY ON THE `<h*>`. The id is the
        only attribute that hoists, and it hoists the same way whether
        it was written (`{#x}`) or slugged from the heading text:
          {a=b .c}      -> <section id="abc"><h1 a="b" class="c">
          # abc
          {a=b .c #x}   -> <section id="x"><h1 a="b" class="c">
          # abc
        Other attributes describe the heading, not the surrounding section.
        See divergence-from-djot.md for the different Djot placement.
      - HEADINGS INSIDE CONTAINERS DO NOT WRAP. The algorithm walks
        TOP-LEVEL blocks (and the sections it opens), so a heading
        inside a blockquote, div, admonition, list item, definition,
        table cell, or footnote definition emits a bare `<h* id="...">`
        with the id ON THE HEADING and no `<section>` around it. A
        `<section>` models THIS document's outline: quoted material
        carries another document's outline, and a div or list item is
        author grouping within one section rather than a new one.
        Everything else about such a heading is unchanged -- it is
        slugged, it shares the one document-order dedup namespace with
        top-level headings (`# abc` then `> # abc` gives `abc` then
        `abc-2`), and it is a valid `</#id>` crossref target. The one
        exception is the implicit `[Heading][]` reference index, which
        declines headings under a blockquote (PART 9R R1).
        When the `sections` option below is off, the top-level shape becomes
        identical to this one, so id
        placement and attribute placement follow ONE rule everywhere in
        the document.
      - Empty document or doc with no headings: zero <section> elements
        are emitted.
      - THE `sections` RENDER OPTION. A host MAY offer a boolean
        `sections` option on the HTML renderer. It defaults to TRUE
        (this section's behavior), and the conformance corpus pins the
        default. When FALSE the renderer emits NO `<section>` element:
        each heading emits `<h{N} id="{slug}">` carrying the id it
        would otherwise have hoisted, alongside its other attributes,
        and the blocks that would have been the section's children stay
        where they are as siblings -- which also means they lose the
        two-space indentation PART 10 §4 gives a container's children,
        because they no longer have that container.
          # A          sections: true      sections: false
          p            <section id="A">    <h1 id="A">A</h1>
                         <h1>A</h1>        <p>p</p>
                         <p>p</p>
                       </section>
        Nothing else changes. Ids, the dedup namespace, `</#id>`
        crossrefs, implicit `[Heading][]` references, `::: toc`,
        endnotes placement (§16), and HeadingNumbers all resolve
        against the SLUG rather than against the element that carries
        it, so they behave identically under either setting. The option
        is HTML-only: no other render target emits `<section>`. It does
        not reach PART 12 §30's interchange `section` node, which is
        content rather than a wrapper derived from a heading run, and
        which HTML emits under either setting.
      - The two-pass id resolution (collect explicit ids first, then
        auto-slug headings with collision-dedup) is unaffected by this
        rule -- only the EMISSION site of the id moves from <h*> to
        <section>. The fragment URL `#{slug}` resolves to <section id> the
        same way browsers resolve to <h* id>.
      - Carve does NOT add `<section>` wrappers around non-heading
        top-level blocks. Headings are the only trigger.

   14. INLINE SPAN VS LINK DISAMBIGUATION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-020] (governs `link`
      and `inline_span` in PART 3). After scanning a bracketed run
      `[ inline_content ]`, the IMMEDIATELY FOLLOWING character selects
      the construct (single-character lookahead, no backtracking):
        - `(` -> inline_link  ([text](url))
        - `[` -> reference_link or collapsed_reference_link
                 ([text][ref] / [text][])
        - `{` -> inline_span  ([text]{attrs})
        - anything else (or end of input) -> the `[`...`]` is LITERAL
          text. Carve has no shortcut reference link: a bare `[label]`
          never resolves against a `[label]: url` definition.
      A MARKDOWN LABEL THAT IS BOTH A CHECKBOX AND A DEFINED REFERENCE IS THE
      CHECKBOX -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-074]. Markdown HAS the shortcut form, so a
      source reading `- [x] done` under a `[x]: /u` definition is ambiguous
      there and not here. The importer answers to cmark-gfm 0.29.0.gfm.13, which
      reads a GFM TASK ITEM and leaves the definition UNUSED. An unused
      definition renders nothing, so an importer MAY write it through or omit
      it; its absence is not a reported loss.
      An inline_span renders as
            <span {attrs}>{inline-rendered content}</span>
      where {attrs} is the attribute block applied verbatim (id, classes,
      key=value), in the same form as any other attribute carrier
      (`#id` -> id, `.x` -> class, `k=v` -> key). The bracketed content
      is full inline content and is parsed recursively, so
      `[a /b/ c]{.x}` -> `<span class="x">a <em>b</em> c</span>`.
      Because the `{` test fires only when the attribute block directly
      abuts `]`, `[text] {.x}` (with a space) is literal `[text]`
      followed by a literal `{.x}` -- the span requires adjacency.
      EMPTY OR INVALID ATTRIBUTE BLOCK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-021]. The directly-abutting
      `{...}` is classified by its content:
        - yields at least one attribute (`[text]{.c}`) -> span with those
          attributes.
        - empty or SPACE-only (`[text]{}`, `[text]{ }`) -> a valid EMPTY
          block: a bare `<span>text</span>`. A TAB inside it is not padding
          and the block is unrecognized (CARVE-P4-001). The empty block is a blessed
          exception -- the bare `attribute_list` production nominally needs
          >= 1 attribute -- so a default-attribute processor can target the
          span.
        - unrecognized non-empty content (`[text]{???}`, `[text]{=y=}`) -> NOT
          an attribute block: the brackets remain literal, and the following
          brace group is still inline-parsed. `[text]{=y=}` ->
          `[text]<mark>y</mark>`, and `[x]{% c %}` -> `[x]`. The inner bracket
          content is still inline-parsed, so `[*x*]{???}` ->
          `[<strong>x</strong>]{???}` (carve#2929).
        - an id or class that is not an `explicit_identifier`, or a key that is not
          an `identifier`, is also unrecognized. A leading ASCII digit is valid
          only for the id/class forms (`{.123}`, `{#1}`), not `{2=v}`.
          A non-identifier character (`{.a!b}`) remains invalid.
          ONE invalid name makes the WHOLE block not an attribute block, even
          when mixed with valid attributes (`{.ok .a!b}`), so the run stays
          literal. No other leading character is newly accepted.
          A digit/`-`/`_` AFTER the first character is valid (`{.a1}`).
      BOOLEAN ATTRIBUTES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-022]. A bare identifier
      (`[text]{featured}`, `{.note open}`, `{disabled}` on a block line) is a
      value-less boolean attribute in source. It has the same empty-string AST
      value as `name=""` and renders `name=""`. It is matched after the
      `key_value_attribute` form (so `k=v` stays a key/value) and mixes freely
      with id/class/key=value in source order; multiple are allowed; a
      digit-first bare word is not a valid name (the block stays literal, as for
      any digit-first identifier). All impls support it (a carve extension
      beyond canonical djot, matching djot-php).
      THE THREE NAMES OF CARVE-P10-003 ARE THE EXCEPTION TO `name=""`: on an
      ordinary span, `abbr`, `time` and `kbd` are consumed into an HTML
      element instead of reaching the output as an attribute, so `[Tab]{kbd}`
      is `<kbd>Tab</kbd>` and not `<span kbd="">Tab</span>`. The OTHER four
      semantic names -- `samp`, `var`, `cite` and `dfn` -- are NOT core: a
      conformant core leaves them as ordinary boolean attributes
      (`<span samp="">x</span>`), and only the Tier-2 SemanticSpan extension
      consumes them (CARVE-P10-008). All seven are boolean attributes everywhere else,
      including in the AST and in the canonical writer, which writes the span
      back as `[Tab]{kbd}` -- an empty-string attribute is spelled bare there
      (CARVE-P11-012).
      A COLON is not an `identifier` character, so a colon-bearing name is
      unrecognized in every form -- `{xml:lang="en"}`, `{.sm:hover}`, `{#a:b}`
      and a bare `{a:b}` all leave the run literal. It stays legal one position
      over, inside an unquoted VALUE (`{k=a:b}`), which `unquoted_value` admits.
      (A `{% ... %}` block is NOT a comment in Carve -- comments are `%%` /
      `%%%` only, PART 9 -- and a `%`-bearing attribute block is an invalid
      attribute that renders literally in ALL impls.)

   15. BLOCK ATTRIBUTE LINES -- OPERATIONAL SEMANTICS (governs
      `block_attributes` in PART 2; matches djot). STATE: pending := the
      ordered list of collected attribute blocks not yet attached.
      A1 COLLECT. A `{…}` alone on a line (the `}` may arrive on a later
         line, A5) that parses as a valid attribute list appends to
         pending and renders NOTHING. It also interrupts an open paragraph
         (§10 I5) and NEVER attaches backward: "Para\n{.class}" ->
         <p>Para</p> (pending then dropped, A4); "Para\n{.class}\n\nNext"
         -> <p>Para</p> then <p class="class">Next</p>.
      A2 FLOAT FORWARD. pending survives blank lines and further attribute
         lines and attaches to the NEXT block element: `{#id}\n\nText` ->
         <p id="id">Text</p>.
      A2a AN INVISIBLE CONSTRUCT IS NOT THE NEXT BLOCK -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-023]. pending
         floats PAST an INVISIBLE LINE and attaches to the next VISIBLE block.
         The class is §10 I5's, AN INVISIBLE LINE IS ONE CLASSIFICATION -- the
         five kinds are enumerated there, and "renders nothing" is its property
         2. This clause is that property's consequence for a floating
         attribute, and nothing more:

             {#i}
             [^f]: note

             e

      A3 MERGE (over all pending blocks, source order):
         * id -> last one wins;
         * key=value -> last value for a given key wins;
         * class -> ALL classes accumulate in source order, DE-DUPLICATED
           ({.a .b} then {.b .c} -> class="a b c"). Accumulating is about
           the LISTS: a later block adds its classes rather than replacing
           the earlier block's, which is what makes stacking work. A class
           already present is not added twice, in one block or across
           several.
         Worked djot-canonical example: {#id} {key=val} {.foo .bar}
         {key=val2} {.baz} {#id2} then "Okay" ->
         <p id="id2" key="val2" class="foo bar baz">Okay</p>.

      A4 DROP IF DANGLING -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-024]. pending with no following block
         element produces no output.

         THERE ARE TWO WAYS TO RUN OUT OF FOLLOWING BLOCKS:

           - END OF DOCUMENT; and
           - END OF THE CONTAINER that holds the attribute.

         A floating attribute is SCOPED to its container. Drop pending
         metadata at document end or the end of its quote, item, definition
         body or footnote body; it MUST NOT attach outside that container.
         `> {.k}` / blank / `tail` leaves `tail` unclassed.

         DROPPING SILENTLY IS THE ONE THING THIS MAY NOT DO. Both cases report
         `unattached-block-attribute` (docs/validation.md), while producing no
         output for the attribute.

         CARVE-P0-009 decides container closure: the attribute leaves no open paragraph,
         so `> {.k}` / `tail` ends the quote and A4 drops the attribute.
         This clause does not decide how an empty container renders.
      A5 MULTI-LINE BLOCK. One block may wrap across lines ({#id\n .foo} is
         one block: id `id`, class `foo`). A BLANK line inside the braces
         ends the attempt -- the text is then literal, not a
         block_attributes. A quoted value containing a literal `}` across
         lines is outside this form.
      A6 NOT AN ATTRIBUTE LIST -> NOT AN ATTRIBUTE LINE. A `{…}` line whose
         braces do not parse as an attribute list is ordinary paragraph
         content under normal inline rules (a `{#a #}` line is an editorial
         comment, §22 family).
      A7 THE ONLY BLOCK CHANNEL. NO block construct takes a trailing `{…}`
         on its own line (djot-strict; headings, fences and `:::` openers
         included) -- the preceding block-attribute line is the sole
         block-level attribute channel. A trailing `{…}` on a heading line
         is literal inline content (PART 2, headings).
      A8 ON A LIST-ITEM MARKER LINE, ALONE MEANS ATTRIBUTE LINE. A
         `-{…} text` with no space after the marker attributes the LIST
         ITEM (PART 2, list items; the marker still needs content, so a
         bare `-{…}` is not a list item at all). THE BLOCK DOES NOT MOVE THE
         ITEM'S CONTENT COLUMN: it is metadata on the item, not marker width;
         `-{.x1} text`, `-{.averylongclass} text` and `- text` all put the
         item body's column 0 at source column 2 (§24 C3). WITH a space, what follows
         the brace run on that line decides:
           - {.c} text      -> literal: <li>{.c} text</li>
           - {a=b .c}      -> an A1 attribute line, floating (A2) to the
             # H               heading below: <li><h1 a="b" class="c" …>
         The discriminator is whether CONTENT FOLLOWS the braces on that
         line, not the column they sit in. Braces trailed by text are part
         of that text; braces alone are a line of their own that happens to
         start after a marker, and A1/A2 apply to it exactly as they would
         at the top level -- a container does not get its own attribute
         rules.
   ============================================================================ *)
